import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int mask = sc.nextInt();
        int a = sc.nextInt();
        int b = sc.nextInt();

        System.out.printf("bit %d set: %b%n", a, ((mask >> a) & 1) == 1);
        System.out.printf("bit %d set: %b%n", b, ((mask >> b) & 1) == 1);
        int result = mask | (1 << a);
        result = result & ~(1 << b);
        System.out.printf("result: %d %s%n", result, bin8(result));
    }

    static String bin8(int x) {
        return String.format("%8s", Integer.toBinaryString(x)).replace(' ', '0');
    }
}

steps.body:
A few one-line tricks come up again and again.

**Power of two.** A power of two has exactly one 1 bit. Subtracting 1 flips that bit and every bit below it, so `n & (n - 1)` is 0 when n has at most one 1 bit. Check `n > 0` first, because 0 has no 1 bits and `Integer.MIN_VALUE` has its only 1 bit in the sign position:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        for (int n : new int[]{8, 12, 1, 0}) {
            {{code}}
            System.out.println(n + ": " + power);
        }
    }
}
```

```java
boolean power = n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0; // 1, 2, 4, 8, ...
```

**Counting bits.** `Integer.bitCount(n)` returns how many bits are 1: `Integer.bitCount(13)` is 3, and `Integer.bitCount(-1)` is 32.

**XOR cancels.** `x ^ x == 0` and `x ^ 0 == x`, and the order of XORs does not matter. XOR every value into a running result that starts at 0: each pair cancels, and what is left is the value that appears an odd number of times.

```java
int result = 0;
for (int x : new int[]{4, 9, 4}) {
    result ^= x;
}
System.out.println(result); // 9
```

That finds the odd one out in one pass with no map or set. It only works when exactly one value has an odd count.

